details.image_prompt:
[
  {
    "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene features a rustic, well-seasoned cast iron skillet placed in the center of the frame, occupying most of the composition with generous negative space around the edges for a cookbook crop. Camera angle is a 3/4 overhead at 40 degrees, ensuring every component in the skillet is clearly visible and identifiable. The depth of field is shallow, keeping the texture of the food in sharp focus while the background is softly blurred. Soft, natural side light streams from a window on the left, casting gentle, natural shadows and creating a slight sheen on the surface of the scallops and the olive oil-glazed vegetables. The skillet rests on an aged, weathered oak wood tabletop with visible grain and natural use marks. Included props: a wooden spoon resting casually against the side of the skillet, and a few scattered cilantro leaves and a discarded, squeezed lime half placed naturally on the wood surface near the skillet. The dish contains a vibrant base of quinoa mixed with charred diced zucchini, red bell pepper, and bright yellow sweet corn, all seasoned with a dusting of deep red smoked paprika. Three perfectly seared, golden-brown sea scallops with visible caramelization and grill marks are placed clearly on top of the quinoa mixture, not submerged or stacked. The dish is finished with a light drizzle of lime juice and a generous scatter of freshly chopped green cilantro. The presentation is authentic, showing enough food for two. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene captures a lived-in, warm, and authentic atmosphere after cooking. Includes slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish, a tiny oil droplet on the skillet rim, and uneven browning on the zucchini. No plastic sheen, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no hands, no faces, indistinguishable from professional editorial food photography."
  }
]
